      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "That would be the expected result in this case.\n\nSquish works be moving transforms / bones closer to each other. So in your case it would move the end point closer to the start bone. And since the end bone doesn't have any vertices weighted to it you won't see any squishing.\n\nTo get the result you are looking for you would need to create a second bone in your chain and weight parts of the mesh to it. You would also need to remove the end position bone. This *should* look the exactly same when it comes to normal rotational movement, but allows you to use squish.\n\nEdit: This is similar to stretch btw, in you case it also wouldn't do anything.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2023-04-28T22:59:08.168Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "1. Have 1 single Weighted Bone with a PhysBone Component.\n\n2. Set a custom Endpoint Position (preferably on the Y-axis).\n\n3. You'll notice that Squish doesn't compress the Bone at all.\n\nThis means that PhysBones will only work properly with Squish or Stretch if there are additional Weighted Bones in the Chain provided from the Model.",
